    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_sdm_311",
      "name": "constraints-evidence-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Will SQLite refuse this row?",
      "description": "Two pieces of evidence from the CodeDev data audit: a cleanup script that deleted a course, and an enrollment saved with a NULL status. Decide whether each statement is true or false.",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The schema was applied once from schema.sql:\n\nCREATE TABLE enrollments (\n    user_id     INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES users (user_id),\n    course_id   INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES courses (course_id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    enrolled_at TEXT NOT NULL,\n    status      TEXT NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, course_id)\n);\n\nCourse 10 still has three enrollments. An admin script runs:\n\nimport sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\"codedev.db\")\ndb.execute(\"DELETE FROM courses WHERE course_id = 10\")\ndb.commit()\n\nStatement: \"The DELETE fails with FOREIGN KEY constraint failed, because the schema declares ON DELETE RESTRICT.\"",
          "explanation": "False. In SQLite, foreign key enforcement is off by default and must be turned on for each connection with PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON (unless the library was compiled with foreign keys on by default, which the standard builds are not). The script never runs the pragma, so the DELETE succeeds and the three enrollments now point to a course that no longer exists. The REFERENCES clause is stored in the schema either way, which is why it looks protected. PRAGMA foreign_key_check lists the orphan rows afterwards. PostgreSQL always enforces foreign keys, so the same script there would fail.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Another version of the table declares the status like this:\n\nstatus TEXT CHECK (status IN ('active', 'completed', 'cancelled'))\n\nThe import script runs:\n\nINSERT INTO enrollments (user_id, course_id, enrolled_at, status)\nVALUES (1, 10, '2026-09-01 10:00:00', NULL);\n\nStatement: \"The insert succeeds and the row is stored with status NULL.\"",
          "explanation": "True. A CHECK constraint only refuses a row when its expression is false. NULL IN ('active', 'completed', 'cancelled') is NULL (unknown), not false, so the row passes the CHECK. SQLite and PostgreSQL both behave this way. To refuse a missing status, the column also needs NOT NULL. The common misconception is that a CHECK listing the allowed values also excludes NULL.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
