      "publicId": "chal_coding_sdm_312",
      "name": "schema-that-refuses-bad-data",
      "type": "mini_coding",
      "title": "A schema that refuses bad data",
      "description": "Write SCHEMA, the CREATE TABLE statements for users, courses and enrollments, so that SQLite itself refuses the bad rows found in the CodeDev data audit. users(user_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, email, name): email required, unique ignoring case and containing '@'; name required. courses(course_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title, level): title required; level required and exactly 'beginner', 'intermediate' or 'advanced'. enrollments(user_id, course_id, enrolled_at, status): user_id and course_id required and must exist in users and courses; deleting a user deletes their enrollments; deleting a course that still has enrollments is refused; enrolled_at required; status required, exactly 'active', 'completed' or 'cancelled', and 'active' when an insert leaves it out; the same (user_id, course_id) pair at most once. Each test is a list of operations, {\"op\": \"insert\", \"table\": ..., \"row\": {...}} or {\"op\": \"delete\", \"table\": ..., \"where\": {...}} or {\"op\": \"select\", \"table\": ..., \"columns\": [...], \"where\": {...}}, which reads stored values back. The runner at the bottom of the file applies SCHEMA, runs PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON, executes the operations one by one and prints a JSON list with one result per operation: \"ok\", or the kind of constraint that refused it, \"not_null\", \"unique\", \"check\" or \"foreign_key\"; for a select, the matching rows as lists of values, such as [[\"active\"]]. Example: [\"ok\", \"unique\", \"check\"].",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "points": 30,
      "coding": {
        "language": "python",
        "filePath": "schema_constraints.py",
        "executionMode": "stdio",
        "timeLimitMs": 3000,
        "memoryLimitMb": 128,
        "scoringMode": "all_or_nothing",
        "maxAttempts": null
      },
      "hints": [
        "For each bad row in the audit, which kind of rule would have stopped it: a missing value, a duplicate, a value outside an allowed list, or a reference to a row that does not exist? Then ask what each of those rules does when the value is NULL.",
        "Everything goes in the column and table definitions: NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK (...), DEFAULT ..., REFERENCES parent (column) ON DELETE ..., and a table-level PRIMARY KEY (a, b). Check three details in the SQLite docs: which collation UNIQUE uses to compare text, whether a CHECK fails when its expression is NULL, and whether a composite PRIMARY KEY in an ordinary SQLite table refuses NULL by itself.",
        "A similar table, not one of yours: CREATE TABLE lessons (lesson_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, course_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES courses (course_id) ON DELETE CASCADE, position INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (position >= 1), title TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE, visibility TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft' CHECK (visibility IN ('draft', 'published')), UNIQUE (course_id, position), UNIQUE (course_id, slug)). Deleting a course deletes its lessons, positions start at 1, a slug is unique per course ignoring case, and a lesson inserted without visibility is a draft."
      ],
